;; The HAMT's bit arithmetic, which was never a primitive.
;;
;; A hash-array-mapped trie reads a hash five bits at a time. Three functions
;; do all of it: take the slice, turn it into a one-bit flag, and count the
;; flags below a given one to get a dense index. That is the whole of it, and
;; none of it touches a host.
;;
;; IT WAS LINKED, NOT GENERATED, and that is why this file exists. `mask`,
;; `bitpos` and `index_of` were written once per runtime and declared to kin as
;; `@kin:link:form:`, alongside genuine primitives like `char-at` and
;; `s-copy-range` which really do need the host's memory. These do not. They
;; sat on the wrong side of the boundary because they are SHORT, which is a bad
;; reason: short is what makes a mistake in them invisible.
;;
;; THE SHIFT IS THE WHOLE RISK. `h` is a hash and its top bit is set half the
;; time. Java's `>>` and C#'s `>>` on a signed int are ARITHMETIC -- they smear
;; the sign down -- so a mask written with `>>` gives 0x1f-and-then-some for
;; exactly the hashes that are hardest to think about, and the map puts an
;; entry in a slot the reader will not look in. All three spellings were
;; checked before this was written and all three were right (`>>>`, `>>>`, and
;; Rust's `>>` on an unsigned). Being right three times by hand is not the same
;; as being right once.
;;
;; `ushr` IS THE ONLY CHOICE HERE. kin has `sar` as well; it is wrong for this
;; and would be silently wrong.
(ns flint.rt.hamt
  (:require [flint.impl.rt :refer [defn return doc
                                   - bit-and shl ushr popcount
                                   I32]]))

(doc "The five bits of `h` that select a slot at `shift`."
     ""
     "NO RECEIVER ANYWHERE IN THIS FILE, which is what makes the bare name"
     "work. `bytehash.kin` takes an unused `rt` at `pow31` and says why: a"
     "receiverless function in a unit that ALSO has methods is emitted inside"
     "Rust's `impl` block, where the bare name is out of scope. A unit with no"
     "receiver at all emits at module level instead -- `hash.kin` is the"
     "precedent, and `valhash.kin` calls its `hash-int` bare across units."
     ""
     "It is also what the callers already write. These three were LINKED, with"
     "templates taking `(h, shift)`, so every call site in `assoc`, `copies`,"
     "`dissoc`, `find` and `merge` passes two arguments. Taking a receiver"
     "would mean editing all of them to hand over an `rt` that none of the"
     "three would read.")
(defn ^:pub ^I32 hash-mask [^I32 h ^I32 shift]
  (return (bit-and (ushr h shift) 31)))

(doc "`hash-mask` as a single set bit: the flag for that slot in a bitmap.")
(defn ^:pub ^I32 bitpos [^I32 h ^I32 shift]
  (return (shl 1 (hash-mask h shift))))

(doc "How many set bits sit BELOW `bit` in `bitmap`."
     ""
     "Which is the dense index of that slot: a HAMT node stores only the"
     "children it has, so the flag's position in the bitmap is not where the"
     "child lives -- the count of flags before it is.")
(defn ^:pub ^I32 index-of [^I32 bitmap ^I32 bit]
  (return (popcount (bit-and bitmap (- bit 1)))))
